Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 01:37:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 0/2] x86/fpu: Allow restoring signal frames with larger xstate_size

This series is a follow-up to "x86/fpu: Restore and reinforce signal
frame portability". While the previous series enabled restoring signal
frames created on machines with fewer xstate features (shrunk
xstate_size), the kernel still enforced that a signal frame's
xstate_size must not exceed the current task's fpstate->user_size.

This restriction prevents migrating a process from a CPU with more
enabled xstate features to a CPU with fewer features, even when the
process has not actively used any of the unsupported features. Because
the actual set of active features is recorded in the XSAVE header
(XSTATE_BV) and validated during restoration, a larger xstate_size is
safe to restore as long as all active features are supported by the
destination CPU and the buffer is large enough for the intersection of
frame and task features.

Patch 1 relaxes the upper-bound check in check_xstate_in_sigframe() and
updates Documentation/arch/x86/xstate.rst accordingly.
Patch 2 extends the sigframe_fpu_portability selftest with valid and
invalid test cases for larger xstate_size frames.

